Yes, in Slack API endpoints, you can tell if a message has been read or not by using the `event` object that is returned in the response. The `event` object contains information about the event that triggered the API call, and one of the properties is `event_timestamp`, which is the timestamp when the event was triggered.

To determine if a message has been read, you can compare the `event_timestamp` with the timestamp when the message was sent. If the `event_timestamp` is later than the message's send timestamp, then the message has been read.

Here's an example of how you can do this in JavaScript:
```javascript
const slackAPI = require('slack');

async function getMessage(channelId, messageId) {
  const client = new slack.WebClient();
  const response = await client.messages.history({
    channel: channelId,
    count: 1,
    message_ts: messageId
  });

  const message = response.messages[0];
  const eventTimestamp = message.event_timestamp;
  const sendTimestamp = message.ts;

  if (eventTimestamp > sendTimestamp) {
    console.log('The message has been read.');
  } else {
    console.log('The message has not been read.');
  }
}

getMessage('C123456', '1234567890');
```
Replace `C123456` with the channel ID and `1234567890` with the message ID you want to check. This code will print "The message has been read." if the message has been read, and "The message has not been read" if the message has not been read.

