Here's a JavaScript function that analyzes the given input data and returns the most relevant protocol:

```javascript
function determineProtocol(inputData) {
  const sourceIP = inputData.sourceIP;
  const destinationIP = inputData.destinationIP;
  const port = inputData.port;
  const payload = inputData.payload;

  if (isIPv6(sourceIP) && isIPv6(destinationIP)) {
    // IPv6 protocol
    return "IPv6";
  } else if (port === 80) {
    // HTTP protocol
    return "HTTP";
  } else if (payload !== undefined) {
    // UDP protocol
    return "UDP";
  } else {
    // No relevant protocol found
    return "None";
  }
}

function isIPv6(ipAddress) {
  const regex = /^([0-9A-Fa-f]{1,4}:){7}([0-9A-Fa-f]{1,4})$/;
  return regex.test(ipAddress);
}
```

This function first checks if both the source and destination IP addresses are in IPv6 format using the `isIPv6()` function. If they are, it returns "IPv6". If the port number is 80, it returns "HTTP". If there is a payload provided, it returns "UDP". Otherwise, it returns "None".

