Here's an Apps Script that searches for a place name in a column and stores the latitude and longitude in other columns:

```javascript
function searchPlace() {
  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSheet();
  var dataRange = sheet.getRange("A2:A"); // Assuming your data starts in cell A2
  var searchTerm = "New York"; // Replace with the term you want to search for

  var results = sheet.createTextFinder(searchTerm).findAll();
  var latLongColumn = 3; // Assuming your latitude and longitude values are in column C (starting from row 2)

  for (var i = 0; i < results.length; i++) {
    var resultRow = results[i].getRow();
    var latLongCell = sheet.getRange(resultRow, latLongColumn);
    var latLongValue = latLongCell.getValue();

    if (latLongValue) {
      var latitude = parseFloat(latLongValue.split(",")[0]);
      var longitude = parseFloat(latLongValue.split(",")[1]);

      sheet.getRange(resultRow, latLongColumn + 1).setValue(latitude); // Store latitude in column D
      sheet.getRange(resultRow, latLongColumn + 2).setValue(longitude); // Store longitude in column E
    }
  }
}
```

This script will search for the term "New York" in column A and store the latitude and longitude values from column C (assuming they are in a comma-separated format) in columns D and E, respectively. You can modify the `searchTerm` variable to search for any term you want, and adjust the `latLongColumn` variable to specify the column where your latitude and longitude values are located.

Here's a PHP custom function named `smart_sort_array` that accepts arguments `set` and `order`. It sorts the elements of the `set` array based on the `age` key of the sub-array, using the specified `order` parameter for sorting.

```php
function smart_sort_array($set, $order) {
    usort($set, function ($a, $b) use ($order) {
        return $order === 'asc' ? strnatcasecmp($a['age'], $b['age']) : strnatcasecmp($b['age'], $a['age']);
    });
    return $set;
}
```

