To inject JavaScript into an existing webpage using your Chrome extension, you can use the `chrome.tabs.executeScript` API. This API allows you to execute a script in the context of a specific tab.

Here's a step-by-step guide on how to set up your boilerplate files and inject JavaScript:

1. Create a new file called `background.js` in your extension folder. This file will contain the code for your background script.
2. In `background.js`, add the following code to load your boilerplate files and inject the JavaScript:
```javascript
chrome.runtime.onInstalled.addListener(function() {
  chrome.tabs.query({active: true, currentWindow: true}, function(tabs) {
    chrome.tabs.executeScript(tabs[0].id, {file: "boilerplate.js"});
  });
});
```
This code listens for the `onInstalled` event, which is triggered when your extension is installed or updated. It then queries the active tab and executes the `boilerplate.js` file in the background script.
3. Create a new file called `boilerplate.js` in your extension folder. This file will contain the code for your boilerplate scripts.
4. In `boilerplate.js`, add the following code to inject the JavaScript into the webpage:
```javascript
document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function() {
  var script = document.createElement("script");
  script.src = chrome.extension.getURL("your_javascript_file.js");
  script.async = true;
  document.body.appendChild(script);
});
```
This code listens for the `DOMContentLoaded` event, which is triggered when the webpage has finished loading. It then creates a new script element and sets its source to the path of your JavaScript file. Finally, it adds the script element to the body of the webpage.
5. Replace `your_javascript_file.js` with the name of your actual JavaScript file that contains the code you want to inject into the webpage.
6. Test your extension by installing it in Chrome and navigating to a webpage where you want to inject the JavaScript. You should see the JavaScript code

