To achieve this task, you'll need to follow these steps:

1. Install the Accord.NET library if you haven't already. You can do this by running `Install-Package Accord.NET` in the Package Manager Console of your Visual Studio project.

2. Load the dataset of labeled sentences. You can use a CSV file or any other suitable format.

3. Preprocess the data by converting it into a matrix of word counts using the CountVectorizer class.

4. Create a Naive Bayes classifier and fit it on the preprocessed data.

5. Use the trained classifier to predict the labels of new observations.

Here's a concise and elegant CSV code that accomplishes this task:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Linq;
using Accord.MachineLearning.Classification;
using Accord.MachineLearning.Vectorization;

namespace TextClassification
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Load the dataset
            var dataset = File.ReadAllLines("dataset.csv").Select(line => line.Split(','));

            // Preprocess the data
            var vectorizer = new CountVectorizer();
            var X = vectorizer.Transform(dataset.Select(row => row.Skip(1)).ToArray());
            var y = dataset.Select(row => row[0]).ToArray();

            // Create a Naive Bayes classifier
            var classifier = new NaiveBayes();

            // Fit the classifier on the preprocessed data
            classifier.Fit(X, y);

            // Predict the labels of new observations
            var newObservations = new[] { "The sports game was exciting" };
            var predictions = classifier.Predict(vectorizer.Transform(newObservations));

            // Output the predicted label
            Console.WriteLine($"Predicted label: {predictions[0]}");
        }
    }
}
```

Make sure to replace "dataset.csv" with the path to your dataset file. This code will load the dataset, preprocess it, create a Naive Bayes classifier, fit

